# incremental-implementation (Cursor)

Build in thin vertical slices instead of implementing an entire feature in
one pass. Each increment leaves the system in a working, testable state.

## The increment cycle

Implement the smallest complete piece → Test (run suite or write one) →
Verify (tests pass, build succeeds, manual check) → Commit with a
descriptive message → move to the next slice, carrying forward rather than
restarting.

## Slicing strategies

- **Vertical (preferred)**: one complete path through the stack per slice
  (e.g. create → list → edit → delete, each shipping working end-to-end
  functionality).
- **Contract-first**: define the API contract, then backend and frontend
  implement against it in parallel, integrate last.
- **Risk-first**: tackle the riskiest/most uncertain piece first so a
  failure is discovered before investing in the rest.

## Rules

- **Simplicity first**: ask "what's the simplest thing that could work?"
  before writing code. Three similar lines beat a premature abstraction.
  Don't build for hypothetical future requirements.
- **Scope discipline**: touch only what the task requires. Don't clean up
  adjacent code, refactor unrelated imports, or add unrequested features.
  Note anything worth improving outside scope instead of fixing it inline.
- **One thing at a time**: each increment changes one logical thing —
  separate commits for a new component vs. a refactor vs. a config change.
- **Keep it compilable**: after every increment the project must build and
  existing tests must pass.
- **Feature flags for incomplete work**: gate anything not ready for users
  so increments can still merge to main.
- **Safe defaults**: new code defaults to conservative, opt-in behavior.
- **Rollback-friendly**: additive changes are easy to revert; keep
  modifications to existing code minimal and focused; don't delete and
  replace something in the same commit.

## After each increment, verify

Does one thing completely; existing tests pass with the repo's own test
command; build succeeds; type-check and lint pass where they exist; new
functionality works as expected; change is committed with a descriptive
message. Don't re-run an unchanged verification command just for
reassurance.

## Red flags

More than ~100 lines written without running tests; multiple unrelated
changes in one increment; "let me just quickly add this too" scope creep;
skipping verify to move faster; build/tests broken between increments;
building abstractions before the third use case demands it.
